      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA) is a lung hypersensitivity disease mediated in part by CD4(+) T(H)2 cells. There is a significant association between ABPA and the HLA-DR2 genotypes DRB1(*)1501 and DRB1(*)1503, whereas resistance might be associated with HLA-DRB1(*)1502."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We sought to elucidate the role of HLA-DR alleles in allergic inflammation in lungs."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "HLA-DR humanized transgenic mice expressing either the susceptible or resistant alleles were analyzed for the nature and extent of pulmonary inflammation after exposure to Aspergillus species antigens."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Exposed DRB1(*)1501 and DRB1(*)1503 transgenic mice displayed infiltrates made up prominently of eosinophils, which is consistent with the inflammation found in ABPA. The resistant DRB1(*)1502 mice, on the other hand, displayed minimal to moderate inflammation, consisting mainly of T-cell infiltrates. Significantly more mucin was produced in the DRB1(*)1503 and DRB1(*)1501 mice, and their ability to limit the number of Aspergillus species conidia within the lung parenchyma was impaired. Despite their differences, both the DRB1(*)1503 and DRB1(*)1502 strains mounted comparable T cell-proliferative responses to Aspergillus species antigens."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The HLA-DR2 alleles DRB1(*)1501 and DRB1(*)1503 play a major role in the development of allergic pulmonary inflammation. In contrast, the HLA-DR2 allele DRB1(*)1502 mediates a nonallergic T(H)1-like response to the organism, possibly explaining an ABPA resistance factor. These results are in support of our published human studies in patients with cystic fibrosis and asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS",
          "content": "HLA-DR typing in patients with cystic fibrosis and asthma will aid in the identification of individuals at risk for ABPA."
        }
      ]},
